WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:10.290
Today's webinar is about using BioSketches
as part of your federal grant application.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:11.730
Sherri Bailey is going to be presenting.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:16.170
And Sherri I'm going to go ahead and turn
it over to you.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:17.350
Thank you, Peter.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:21.619
Again my name is Sherri and I would like to
thank you for joining us.

00:00:21.619 --> 00:00:33.420
Our objective today is to create biographical
sketches for NIH, NSF and DOE federal grant

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:34.530
applications.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:39.280
Before we really get started let’s have
a quick poll.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:49.910
One of the things we would like to know is
which federal agency you will be using.

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:53.970
It looks like the majority of you will be
using NIH.

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:58.550
There are quite a few of you here today using
NSF, 13 percent using other agencies, and

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:06.230
some people haven’t decided if they are
going to submit.

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:11.149
Okay, let’s go ahead and jump right in.

00:01:11.149 --> 00:01:17.840
SciENcv and My Bibliography are a part of
a free profile service that is part of My

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:24.500
NCBI which is offered by NIH, the National
Institutes of Health.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:28.880
One of the benefits of a free NCBI account
includes a professional profile tool along

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:33.220
with other great features to use with NCBI
services.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:38.520
Links will be provided at the end of the presentation
if you would like to learn more.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:42.530
But today our focus is My Bibliography for
biosketch creation.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:45.039
What exactly is SciENcv.

00:01:45.039 --> 00:01:52.420
SciENcv allows you to document your education,
employment, research activities, and incorporate

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:58.899
your publications, honors, research grants,
and other professional contributions.

00:01:58.899 --> 00:02:03.900
A few of the benefits include the ability
to leverage data from existing systems that

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:13.610
you may already use like ORCiD, reduce administrative
burden by creating a document once to copy

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:20.080
and edit for multiple grant applications,
and take comfort in knowing that your documents

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.910
will adhere government standards.

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:31.220
When creating a biosketch, you have 3 options:
Create from scratch, duplicating an existing

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:39.030
document and modifying OR using an external
source like ORCiD to import citations and

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:40.030
education.

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:46.290
My Bibliography is a reference tool that helps
you save your citations from PubMed.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:54.460
If you do not have citations in PubMed, you
can manually upload a RIS file, created by

00:02:54.460 --> 00:03:00.620
your citation manager and enter the citation
information using the built in templates.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:06.540
An additional benefit of using My Bibliography
is that it can generate a public URL to share

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:10.450
your Bibliography with others.

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:14.890
Now that we have a general understanding of
the tools used to create a biosketch.

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:20.470
Let’s touch briefly on how to create an
account to access SciENcv.

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:31.720
There are two ways: NSF users, you may want
to google SciENcv using your favorite web

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:32.730
browser.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:39.480
From here if you have an eRA Commons or NSF
credentials select the appropriate icon and

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:42.320
and provide your login information.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:49.450
If your institution is an InCommon partner
or would prefer to use Google for example

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:53.380
click the See more 3rd party sign in options.

00:03:53.380 --> 00:04:00.480
The final option is the NCBI icon where you
can create an account to log in directly to

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:05.700
the site which allows you to use the forgot
my password feature.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:14.790
The second way is to access your My NCBI account
and SciENcv is to go to PubMed.gov or any

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:20.879
other NCBI page and click the log in link
located on the top right which will provide

00:04:20.879 --> 00:04:24.009
similar creation and log in options.

00:04:24.009 --> 00:04:30.500
After your account has been created or at
any time you can link your 3rd party accounts

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:38.759
such as ORCiD, eRA Commons, HRA (Health Research
Alliance) and/or NSF.

00:04:38.759 --> 00:04:43.370
You may also link your account to Google or
your institution (if it is listed) which will

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:48.539
provide additional ways to access the single
account.

00:04:48.539 --> 00:04:51.199
Linking is as simple as 1, 2, 3.

00:04:51.199 --> 00:04:57.229
To link your account click on your username
located on the top right of the screen, locate

00:04:57.229 --> 00:05:04.669
the Linked accounts field and click the change
button, locate and select your institution

00:05:04.669 --> 00:05:08.400
or Google and provide credentials.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.419
Another feature located in your account settings
is delegations.

00:05:13.419 --> 00:05:18.500
As a researcher, you may not always have time
to manage your account.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:25.930
My NCBI users can grant access to others to
edit and create documents in SciENcv as well

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:30.460
as add, edit and delete citations in My Bibliography.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:32.889
Now it’s time for a quick poll.

00:05:32.889 --> 00:05:40.990
I would like to know how you will be using
delegations within your My NCBI account.

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:46.789
It looks like the majority of you create and
maintain yourself.

00:05:46.789 --> 00:05:52.289
And it’s almost the same percent for those
who maintain Biosketches for someone else.

00:05:52.289 --> 00:05:56.400
This is a great option for you to use.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:02.050
To access this feature, click your username
located on the top right of the screen, go

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:07.980
to Add a delegate, type in the email address
of the person managing your account and click

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:08.980
ok.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:14.559
The delegate will receive an email which invites
them to create their own account in order

00:06:14.559 --> 00:06:17.099
to manage yours.

00:06:17.099 --> 00:06:20.050
Now that we have the administration piece
out of the way.

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:23.000
Let’s begin creating a biosketch.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:28.370
The first time you access SciENcv you will
be taken to the create a new document page.

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:33.469
The form will allow you to: Give the document
a title.

00:06:33.469 --> 00:06:35.160
Determine the format of the biosketch.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.999
Select your data source.

00:06:37.999 --> 00:06:43.900
Decide if you will be starting with a blank
document, duplicating an existing document,

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:47.860
or using an external source like ORCiD.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:54.819
Remember ORCiD will automatically import your
biographical and citation data into the biosketch.

00:06:54.819 --> 00:07:02.389
Finally, you determine if you would like to
share your document via a URL or keep it private.

00:07:02.389 --> 00:07:06.550
You always have the ability to change the
setting at any time.

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:13.280
Once you click create, you are ready to enter
information into the fields.

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:17.830
All biosketches have the following components
in common:

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:23.460
The profile name or title that you created
on the previous page,

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:30.219
The profile type either NIH, NIH Fellowship,
IES or NSF,

00:07:30.219 --> 00:07:35.569
The last time you updated the file,
The ability to share,

00:07:35.569 --> 00:07:42.110
And over on the right download options and
OMB information.

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:47.620
Please note that NSF documents: the biosketch
and the Current and Pending Support document

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:55.199
only have the PDF and XML versions available
which is a requirement in NSF’s Proposal

00:07:55.199 --> 00:07:57.409
&amp; Award Policies &amp; Procedures Guide.

00:07:57.409 --> 00:08:05.360
The next field provides your information,
including your name and the linked accounts

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:11.009
of eRA Commons, NSF and ORCiD if available.

00:08:11.009 --> 00:08:17.900
Which brings us to the first section of education.

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:26.339
Although the titles may appear different,
and section A for NSF, the data requested

00:08:26.339 --> 00:08:27.339
is the same.

00:08:27.339 --> 00:08:32.830
Here you will enter degree and training information.

00:08:32.830 --> 00:08:39.060
Once the information has been added, the summary
appears on the screen, with the ability to

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:42.560
delete or edit the entry.

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:52.380
This is an example for NSF, but the education
and training fields for NIH and IES Biosketches

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:55.790
appear the same.

00:08:55.790 --> 00:09:02.180
You are probably wondering what happened to
Section A for NIH and IES Biosketches.

00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:07.610
Here it consists of a personal statement with
the option to include up to 4 peer reviewed

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:08.610
publications.

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:13.100
It is important to note that a lot of the
information can be reused but there are subtle

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:20.870
differences between NIH and IES from NSF biosketches.

00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:28.769
Section B requests employment and appointment
information which are also similar.

00:09:28.769 --> 00:09:33.970
But NIH &amp; IES requests additional information.

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:38.630
Remember to always read the directions provided
beneath each section so that you are providing

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:41.610
exactly what is requested.

00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:48.209
Here is an example of section B for NIH &amp; IES.

00:09:48.209 --> 00:09:57.089
Here is an example of section B for NSF.

00:09:57.089 --> 00:10:02.940
Section C is where you are able to provide
a description, in your own words, your contributions

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:05.970
to science and add supporting citations.

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:13.160
You will notice in the box below, the ability
to add a link to include your complete list

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:19.460
of published work from My Bibliography which
will appear in your downloaded document for

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:24.449
NIH and IES biosketches.

00:10:24.449 --> 00:10:32.459
Section C for NSF biosketches provides a description
of acceptable and unacceptable products and

00:10:32.459 --> 00:10:34.779
citations to be included.

00:10:34.779 --> 00:10:44.960
There is also a unique element to NSF which
includes the ability to add additional citations.

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:54.740
For section C all of the biosketches have
an add citations box that looks and operates

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:58.509
the same.

00:10:58.509 --> 00:11:00.139
The first tab is My Bibliography.

00:11:00.139 --> 00:11:04.920
Beneath that, in the blue box, you will see
Add citations.

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:12.970
Add citations will take you directly to PubMed
to add citations to My Bibliography.

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:20.350
When you click on Go to My Bibliography, it
will take you to, yes you guessed it, My Bibliography.

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:29.279
From here you are able to add citations from
PubMed, add citations from a file, which includes

00:11:29.279 --> 00:11:39.029
RIS from examples like EndNote and RefWorks,
or add citations manually using My Bibliography.

00:11:39.029 --> 00:11:44.700
Once that is complete you can go back, by
clicking on your username located on the top

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:48.990
right, and selecting Dashboard.

00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:52.980
You may have noticed that it looks a bit different
from the first time you saw the dashboard.

00:11:52.980 --> 00:12:00.149
Yes, you are able to customize the dashboard
to either collapse, move or hide windows.

00:12:00.149 --> 00:12:06.370
If you wish to restore any of the items, click
the Customize this page link near the top,

00:12:06.370 --> 00:12:08.970
and select the items to restore.

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:13.910
Now let’s continue with the biosketch by
either clicking directly on the document title

00:12:13.910 --> 00:12:16.940
or by clicking the Manage SciENcv link.

00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:27.509
Once we are back into the Biosketch, it will
look like this after information is added

00:12:27.509 --> 00:12:30.019
to My Bibliography.

00:12:30.019 --> 00:12:37.370
If you have linked your ORCiD information,
the second tab will populate as well.

00:12:37.370 --> 00:12:41.020
We would like to know if you currently use
ORCiD.

00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:46.250
If you could take anotner poll, we would appreciate
it.

00:12:46.250 --> 00:12:50.910
It looks like the majority of you do use ORCiD.

00:12:50.910 --> 00:12:56.210
For those of you who are not familiar with
ORCiD it is a non-profit organization that

00:12:56.210 --> 00:13:01.480
provides a unique identifier that distiinguishes
you from other researchers.

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:09.079
So you usually put this on your publications,
but you can also use to put patents and products

00:13:09.079 --> 00:13:12.149
that you have created, as well.

00:13:12.149 --> 00:13:20.209
Moving on, Section D for NIH and IES is where
you are able to associate your awards with

00:13:20.209 --> 00:13:21.380
the biosketch.

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:29.500
For the NIH Fellowship biosketch, awards and
scholastic performance can be added, which

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:31.399
is towards the bottom.

00:13:31.399 --> 00:13:38.520
For NSF users you will use a completely different
forma, which is also part of SciENcv, which

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:39.980
is current and pending support.

00:13:39.980 --> 00:13:47.730
This is where you add your awards as well
as any in-kind contributions.

00:13:47.730 --> 00:13:55.029
Section D for NSF is Synergistic Activities,
which provides a box for free text.

00:13:55.029 --> 00:14:00.589
Once the information is entered, you have
the ability to hoover over an entry and change

00:14:00.589 --> 00:14:05.529
the position of the text, delete or edit the
information.

00:14:05.529 --> 00:14:12.959
Once you have verified the information and
ready to save or print, the PDF links are

00:14:12.959 --> 00:14:16.769
available on the top and lower right-hand
side.

00:14:16.769 --> 00:14:21.710
As well as the XML, for all users.

00:14:21.710 --> 00:14:28.470
The only difference is, again, NSF users are
not able to download the Word document.

00:14:28.470 --> 00:14:34.430
If you find you made an error, go back into
your document, make the corrections and resave

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:35.430
or print.

00:14:35.430 --> 00:14:42.899
In summary, SciEncv allows you to create and
store multiple documents from scratch, an

00:14:42.899 --> 00:14:52.779
external data source or you are able to copy
your form from an existing biosketch of C&amp;PS

00:14:52.779 --> 00:14:55.470
form, which is NSF.

00:14:55.470 --> 00:15:00.670
You are able to output your PDF or Word document
depending on which one you are using, on demand.

00:15:00.670 --> 00:15:06.040
And you have the ability to have delegates
to manage your account.

00:15:06.040 --> 00:15:11.950
Documentation about SciENcv, My Bibliography
and My NCBI can be found here.

00:15:11.950 --> 00:15:17.259
Also at the bottom is an email address, info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov,
where we welcome feedback, suggestions, questions

00:15:17.259 --> 00:15:21.040
and help for any questions that may arise.

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:23.760
Let’s open the floor up to any additional
questions.

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:25.540
[Peter Cooper] Thanks Sherri.

00:15:25.540 --> 00:15:27.939
Rana, are there any questions?

00:15:27.939 --> 00:15:32.550
[Rana Morris] There are several.

00:15:32.550 --> 00:15:36.910
So Sherri, one of the participants in this
webinar is interested in using the bio sketch

00:15:36.910 --> 00:15:44.340
but goes directly to funding sources, both
NIH and NSF.

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:52.199
Is it possible through SciENcv to create separate
Biosketches for NIH and NSF and what if they

00:15:52.199 --> 00:15:56.779
are applying to two or three grant programs
from either of these institutions?

00:15:56.779 --> 00:16:02.089
Can use my bibliography to create multiple
bio sketches.

00:16:02.089 --> 00:16:09.149
[Sherri Bailey] Within NSF you're able to
use the PDF which they supply on their form,

00:16:09.149 --> 00:16:12.120
as well is the bio sketch within SciENcv.

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:18.019
It's going to be mandated by NSF beginning
October fifth.

00:16:18.019 --> 00:16:27.290
The fact that you can interchange the information
because each policy, NSF, as well is NIH has

00:16:27.290 --> 00:16:33.000
their own particular guidelines that's why
there are slight differences but we do give

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:35.620
you the ability to share the information.

00:16:35.620 --> 00:16:37.600
In order to create multiple bio sketches.

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:38.600
[Rana] Excellent.

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:45.749
A number of people are interested in ORCiD
specifically and they know you can import

00:16:45.749 --> 00:16:51.689
ORCiD citations into my bibliography to be
used for SciENcv.

00:16:51.689 --> 00:16:57.550
Can you do the inverse, take My bib and reroute
them back to ORCiD.

00:16:57.550 --> 00:17:02.069
Is there a better way to do it, is one more
effective than the other?

00:17:02.069 --> 00:17:04.459
[sherri] At this time no.

00:17:04.459 --> 00:17:06.180
We just import their information.

00:17:06.180 --> 00:17:12.620
Were looking at other ways they can retrieve
information from us to make it better for

00:17:12.620 --> 00:17:13.910
users.

00:17:13.910 --> 00:17:18.390
[Rana] As a follow-up on that.

00:17:18.390 --> 00:17:24.760
They want to know if there is a direct [indescernible]
at SciENcv or a possibility to import or export

00:17:24.760 --> 00:17:28.640
bib text format in my bibliography.

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:30.450
Do you know about that?

00:17:30.450 --> 00:17:32.690
[Sherri] Were looking at that way as well.

00:17:32.690 --> 00:17:42.549
If you take all your suggestions and write
to info at NCBI dot NLM dot NIH dot GOV.

00:17:42.549 --> 00:17:48.340
The more documentation we have that this is
what people want the faster we can probably

00:17:48.340 --> 00:17:50.360
get this into production.

00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:51.520
[Rana] Great.

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:59.520
You may not know but is there any particular
place that provides advice for good quality

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:00.750
bio sketches.

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:02.790
What funders are looking for?

00:18:02.790 --> 00:18:07.900
I imagine each funding agency might have its
own documentation but there are number of

00:18:07.900 --> 00:18:10.570
people who asked about guidance.

00:18:10.570 --> 00:18:15.140
What would you recommend in terms of them
finding guidance about how to create a bio

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:18.720
sketch for particular agency or best practices.

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:25.200
[Sherri] I would go back to each agency to
ask your question.

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:27.850
There are specific things they are looking
for.

00:18:27.850 --> 00:18:30.240
[Rana] Here is a question.

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:37.640
If you have a current bio sketch and want
to import your BioSketch into SciENcv is it

00:18:37.640 --> 00:18:38.960
possible?

00:18:38.960 --> 00:18:42.590
[Sherri] It is not possible at this time.

00:18:42.590 --> 00:18:46.240
It is definitely something I'm taking note
of.

00:18:46.240 --> 00:18:50.080
We will put in front of our development team.

00:18:50.080 --> 00:18:56.380
[Rana] And finally, in terms of exporting
bio sketches, are their different formats

00:18:56.380 --> 00:18:57.380
for exporting.

00:18:57.380 --> 00:19:05.560
Say you finally created your biosketch for
NIH or NSF, what format does SciENcv export

00:19:05.560 --> 00:19:08.320
their bio sketches in?

00:19:08.330 --> 00:19:15.770
[Sherri] You have the ability for all of them,
either PDF as well as an XML file.

00:19:15.770 --> 00:19:24.420
For IES as well is NIH BioSketch you have
the additional word document that you're able

00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:26.260
to manipulate.

00:19:26.700 --> 00:19:27.200
 

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:27.740
[Rana] Okay.

00:19:27.740 --> 00:19:28.440
Great.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:31.020
Thank you very much for attending our webinar.

00:19:31.020 --> 00:19:34.220
We hope you have a great and safe rest of your day.

00:19:34.240 --> 00:19:35.060
Thank you.

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:36.200
[Peter] Thanks Rana

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:37.200
And thanks Sherri.

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:37.820
This is Peter Cooper again.

00:19:37.820 --> 00:19:38.320
Thanks everybody and I'm going to end the webinar

00:19:38.320 --> 00:19:38.820
 

00:19:38.820 --> 00:19:39.320
 

00:19:39.320 --> 00:19:39.820
 

00:19:39.820 --> 00:19:41.500
I'm going to and the webinar.

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:42.860
[event concluded]

